  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The guests realize the recipe is in French, and Louis watches Max embarrass himself. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Тут все по-французски.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Для нашего шефа это не проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Правда, Максимилиан?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Си.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Си? Идиот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Си — это по-итальянски.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "По-французски будет уи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, конечно, я знаю это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Максимилиан, вы понимаете французский язык?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно, я прекрасно понимаю французский.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Ну, что ты будешь делать теперь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я прочитаю этот рецепт и приготовлю блюдо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Это будет очень смешно.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Тут все по-французски.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here everything is in French.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'here' straight to 'in French'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Для нашего шефа это не проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For our chef this is not a problem.' Notice the word order: 'chef' comes first, and 'problem' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Правда, Максимилиан?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Right, Maximilian?' Elena puts 'honestly' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'person name'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Си.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Si.' A compact expression built around 'oui' and 'oui'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Си? Идиот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Si? Idiot.' The key word here means 'oui', which Louis combines with 'idiot'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Си — это по-итальянски.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Si is in Italian.' Louis starts with 'oui' and pairs it with 'this' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "По-французски будет уи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In French it will be oui.' Louis puts 'in French' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'will be'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, конечно, я знаю это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, of course, I know this.' The key word here means 'of course', which Max combines with 'I'. Elena answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Максимилиан, вы понимаете французский язык?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maximilian, do you understand the French language?' Here 'person name' sets up the idea, while 'language' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно, я прекрасно понимаю французский.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course, I understand French perfectly.' You'll hear 'of course' all the time in the show, here connected with 'French'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Ну, что ты будешь делать теперь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, what will you do now?' In Russian, 'what' leads into 'now' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я прочитаю этот рецепт и приготовлю блюдо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will read this recipe and prepare the dish.' Listen for the word meaning 'I' right alongside 'to read' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Это будет очень смешно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This will be very funny.' The first content word means 'this', and 'ridiculous' rounds out the thought. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
